const QUESTIONS = [
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A pipeline must fetch a report, summarize it, and email the summary — the same three steps, in the same order, every single run. Which architecture fits?",o:["A manager/subagent hierarchy coordinating three separate specialists","A predefined workflow, since the sequence and order are fixed and known ahead of time","A single call with adaptive thinking enabled and no other structure","An autonomous agent that re-derives the three steps on each run so it can adapt to failures"],c:[1],e:"When the steps and their order are already known, code can hard-code the sequence — that predictability is exactly what a workflow buys you."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A research assistant needs to investigate a claim, and the number of searches and follow-up questions it needs can't be known until it sees intermediate results. What fits best?",o:["An agent that decides its own next step in a loop based on what it finds","A workflow with exactly one hard-coded branch point","A fixed three-step workflow written in advance","A fixed workflow that runs a set number of searches and then summarizes them"],c:[0],e:"When the path genuinely depends on results you can't predict beforehand, only a model deciding step-by-step can adapt."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Compared with a workflow, what do you generally give up by choosing an agent?",o:["Fine-grained control over cost, since the number of calls is decided at runtime rather than up front","The ability to use the same underlying models, since agent loops run on a restricted subset of them","Predictability and ease of testing, because the exact sequence of steps can vary between runs","Compatibility with the underlying Messages API"],c:[2],e:"Agents trade a fixed, testable path for the flexibility to adapt — that's the core cost of choosing dynamic control flow."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A team builds a fully autonomous agent for a task that, on reflection, only ever follows one of two known paths depending on a single upfront classification. What's the likely consequence?",o:["The task takes noticeably longer, since each run re-derives the classification first","The agent handles both known paths but cannot be extended to a third one later","Test coverage improves, since each run exercises a different path through the system","Reduced testability, since the path is re-decided at runtime rather than fixed in code"],c:[3],e:"When the branching is bounded and known in advance, an agent gives up a fixed, assertable path without gaining any adaptability the task actually needs."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A coordinator agent hands a market-research subtask to one subagent and a technical-feasibility subtask to another, then combines both write-ups into one report. What pattern is this?",o:["A routing pattern, where a classifier forwards the request to one specialist","A sequential chain, where each subtask consumes the output of the previous one","An evaluator-optimizer loop, where one agent critiques another agent's draft","An orchestrator-workers pattern, where a lead agent delegates and then integrates"],c:[3],e:"Delegating distinct subtasks in parallel and integrating the results is orchestrator-workers; the alternatives describe chaining, critique loops, and routing instead."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Delegating work to specialized subagents pays off most clearly when subtasks are:",o:["Trivial enough that a single prompt already handles them well","Tightly coupled, so splitting them would mean passing most of the context to each part anyway","Separable by domain or independent enough to run in parallel","Guaranteed to require exactly one tool call each"],c:[2],e:"Orchestration earns its overhead when work can genuinely be split — by expertise, by independence, or both."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Why might a manager agent deliberately isolate each subagent's working context from its own?",o:["So each subagent can be run on a cheaper model tier than the manager uses","So results arrive sooner, since a smaller context is faster for the model to read","So the manager can replay a subagent's full reasoning when auditing a result","So a subagent's exploratory dead ends don't dilute the manager's own context"],c:[3],e:"Isolation exists to keep a subtask's noisy exploration out of the manager's context; tier choice, auditability, and latency are separate concerns."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A manager agent gives every subagent read access to the manager's entire running context, including unrelated prior subtasks. What's the most likely downside?",o:["Better consistency, since every subagent now works from exactly the same shared facts","Context bloat and cross-task interference from irrelevant carried-over history","Higher cost per subagent call, while the manager's own context stays entirely unchanged","Faster responses, since each subagent can answer from history rather than re-deriving it"],c:[1],e:"Sharing everything defeats the point of scoping a subagent to its own subtask, and the extra history competes with the content that actually matters."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"What does the Claude Agent SDK add on top of calling the Messages API directly yourself?",o:["A managed hosting layer that runs the finished agent on Anthropic's own infrastructure","Mandatory hosting of your application on Anthropic's infrastructure","Scaffolding for the agent loop itself — tool execution, context, and turn-taking","A replacement for the Messages API that uses a different underlying model"],c:[2],e:"The SDK sits on top of the same API, providing the loop mechanics rather than replacing the API or the need for good prompts."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A team building a custom agent loop by hand — parsing stop reasons, dispatching tool calls, reassembling context — is essentially reimplementing:",o:["A capability that doesn't exist anywhere in Anthropic's tooling","What the Claude Agent SDK already provides as scaffolding","The Message Batches API's internal request queue","MCP's resource-listing protocol"],c:[1],e:"That loop-management logic is precisely what the Agent SDK is meant to save you from writing yourself."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"In an agent loop, a 'hook' is best described as:",o:["A point in the loop's lifecycle where you can intercept or block an action","A parameter that selects which model tier handles the request","A caching mechanism that stores repeated prompt prefixes","A stored prompt fragment reused across turns to reduce repeated token processing"],c:[0],e:"Hooks are lifecycle interception points, distinct from caching, model selection, or UI concerns."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A hook is configured to require confirmation before any tool call that deletes data can execute. What is this hook doing?",o:["Retrying the deletion automatically whenever the first attempt returns an error","Recording the deletion in an audit log once the tool has already carried it out","Gating a specific class of destructive action at a defined lifecycle point before it runs","Rewriting the delete request into a safer, reversible operation before it executes"],c:[2],e:"This is a pre-execution gate. Logging happens after the fact, and neither rewriting nor retrying describes blocking an action pending confirmation."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A separate hook records every tool call an agent makes, with no ability to block anything. What purpose does this hook serve?",o:["Determining whether the task is better suited to a workflow or an agent","Selecting which model tier is used for each call","Observability — an audit trail of what the agent did, without changing outcomes","The exact same purpose as a hook that blocks destructive actions before they run"],c:[2],e:"A passive logging hook and an active blocking hook serve different purposes even though both are lifecycle interceptions."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Which best summarizes the basic loop most tool-using agent implementations follow?",o:["Send a request, check why it stopped, execute any requested tool, and repeat until done","Send one request and return the text it produces, treating any tool request as a completed answer","Stream partial tokens to the user with tool calls disabled for the whole session","Batch every request overnight regardless of how urgent the task is"],c:[0],e:"The defining shape of an agent loop is checking the stop reason and looping on tool execution until the model is actually done."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Why should a production agent loop cap the number of tool-call iterations or set an overall timeout?",o:["A timeout lowers per-token cost, since shorter requests bill at a reduced rate","Capping iterations is the mechanism that enforces least privilege over tools","The bound is what signals to the model how many steps it ought to plan for","Without a bound, a misbehaving loop can keep calling tools indefinitely"],c:[3],e:"A cap is a safety net against runaway loops. It isn't a planning hint, a permissions mechanism, or a pricing lever."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A graph-based orchestration library (in the style of LangGraph) is most useful compared to a plain linear workflow when:",o:["The task has genuine branches or loops a straight-line sequence can't cleanly express","The workflow's steps are fixed, but you would prefer to express them in a declarative format","The application has no need for state to persist between steps","The task is a strictly linear sequence with no conditional logic anywhere"],c:[0],e:"Graph orchestration earns its added complexity once you need conditional branching or looping beyond a simple line of steps."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"How should a team building on Claude generally think about third-party orchestration frameworks like LangGraph?",o:["As a mandatory dependency that every Claude application must include","As an optional layer for structuring multi-step coordination","As a type of MCP server that ships tools and resources","As a direct, drop-in replacement for the Messages API itself"],c:[1],e:"These frameworks are optional coordination layers built on top of the API, not a requirement or a protocol like MCP."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A support-triage system sometimes needs to answer directly, sometimes escalate to a human, and sometimes call a refund tool — and which path is right isn't knowable until the ticket is read. This case favors:",o:["A fixed workflow that escalates first, then falls back to answering if no human is available","An agent that can choose among those paths per ticket","Processing every ticket through the Message Batches API exclusively","Disabling tool use so the model can only produce text"],c:[1],e:"Per-instance unpredictability in which path is correct is a defining case for letting the model decide rather than hard-coding a path."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A workflow contains four fixed steps, but step 3 branches to one of two fixed sub-paths based on a classification made in step 2. Is this still reasonably called a workflow?",o:["No — any conditional branch means the model is choosing the path, which defines an agent","Yes — a small, bounded set of branches doesn't turn a workflow into a full agent","Yes, but only if the branching step also calls an external tool to decide","Yes, but only because there are exactly two sub-paths rather than three or more"],c:[1],e:"What distinguishes a workflow is that the set of possible paths is fixed at design time, not that it contains exactly one path."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Why is an agent's behavior generally harder to write deterministic tests against than a workflow's?",o:["Agent responses tend to be longer, which makes precise assertions harder to write","Agents cannot be run against a fixed input, since each session builds its own prompt","Agent tool calls hit live systems, so the tests need network access in order to run","The path the agent takes can differ between runs based on the model's own decisions"],c:[3],e:"Run-to-run variation in the path taken is the core obstacle; the other issues are real testing annoyances but apply to workflows too."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"All else equal, how does an agent's cost typically compare to a workflow's for the same underlying task?",o:["Workflows are cheaper by construction, since their step count is capped at design time","Agent cost is fixed in advance regardless of how many tool calls happen","It depends mainly on how many LLM calls actually get made, not the architecture label","Agents are inherently and unconditionally more expensive purely by virtue of being agents"],c:[2],e:"Cost tracks actual usage — call count and size — not whether you've labeled the thing a workflow or an agent."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A subagent in an orchestrator pattern typically operates:",o:["With unrestricted visibility into every other subagent's full history by default","Scoped to the specific piece of work it was delegated, often with its own isolated working context","With the manager's full conversation history attached, so that it can always follow the wider goal of the task","Without any way to return a result back to the manager"],c:[1],e:"Subagents are scoped delegates — bounded to their subtask and typically isolated, not given free rein over everything else."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"An orchestrator dispatches three subagents to work in parallel, but the manager has no defined strategy for merging their three outputs into one answer. What's missing?",o:["A fixed workflow, which is required before any subagent can run at all","Nothing — parallel subagents automatically merge their own outputs with no coordination needed","A result-aggregation step — delegation alone doesn't produce a coherent final answer","Tool access for the subagents, which is unrelated to how their outputs get combined"],c:[2],e:"Orchestration needs both delegation and a deliberate integration step; skipping the second leaves you with fragments, not an answer."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"When is a manager/subagent hierarchy probably overkill for a task?",o:["Whenever the task involves any external data retrieval at all","When the task is small enough for one agent to handle directly","Whenever more than one tool is available to the agent","Whenever the response needs to be streamed to a user interface"],c:[1],e:"Orchestration overhead is only worth paying when there's real work to split; a small, undivided task doesn't need a manager layer."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"What should primarily drive the choice between building a workflow and building an agent for a given task?",o:["Defaulting to a workflow, on the basis that predictability generally outweighs adaptability","Whichever approach the team already has the most experience building, since familiarity lowers delivery risk","Always defaulting to an agent, since more autonomy is inherently better in every case","How predictable and bounded the actual task is, not a blanket preference for one architecture over the other"],c:[3],e:"Matching architecture to task shape — not a fixed default in either direction — is the sound approach."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A subagent finishes a delegated research task and needs to hand its findings back to the manager agent. How does this typically happen?",o:["The subagent returns a result that the manager folds into its own context","Results can only be delivered by disabling context isolation beforehand","The subagent must call the Message Batches API to deliver its result","The subagent's entire isolated context automatically merges into the manager's context afterward"],c:[0],e:"The point of isolation is that only the useful result crosses back, not the subagent's full exploratory context."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"Sequential subagent delegation (each subagent starts only after the previous one finishes) differs from parallel delegation mainly in that sequential delegation:",o:["Costs less overall, since work is not duplicated across concurrently running subagents","Removes the need to aggregate results, since each subagent extends the previous output","Uses less context overall, since only one subagent is ever active at a given moment","Trades speed for letting each later subagent build on earlier results"],c:[3],e:"The defining tradeoff is latency in exchange for later steps being able to depend on earlier ones; the rest don't follow from ordering alone."},
{d:"D1",t:"s",q:"A vague, one-line tool description on a subagent's only available tool causes the subagent to call it incorrectly. Whose responsibility is that, architecturally?",o:["The tool's design — poor descriptions degrade tool-selection accuracy regardless of caller","It can only happen inside a manager/subagent hierarchy, never in a single-agent setup","It's unrelated to tool design and is purely a model-tier problem","It is specific to server-side tools, since their schemas are defined outside your codebase"],c:[0],e:"Tool-description quality affects correct invocation everywhere tools are used, not just in orchestrated multi-agent setups."},
{d:"D1",t:"m",q:"Which two situations most clearly favor a fixed, code-orchestrated workflow over an agent? (Select 2)",o:["Deterministic, repeatable testing of the whole sequence is a priority","The exact steps and their order are already known and repeat identically each run","Maximum adaptability to genuinely unanticipated situations is the primary goal","The number of steps needed depends on results that are only known once the task starts"],c:[0,1],e:"Known, repeatable steps and a need for deterministic testing both point toward a workflow; the other two are classic agent triggers."},
{d:"D1",t:"m",q:"Which two are legitimate reasons to use a manager/subagent orchestration pattern rather than a single agent? (Select 2)",o:["Cost needs to be minimized regardless of whether delegation actually helps","Subtasks require distinct domain expertise that's easier to isolate per subagent","Subtasks can run independently of one another and benefit from parallel execution","The task has exactly one step and no meaningful subdivision is possible"],c:[1,2],e:"Orchestration pays off when subtasks are genuinely separable by expertise or parallelizable — not when there's nothing to divide."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Instructions that should apply from the very first turn of a conversation belong where in a Messages API request?",o:["In a `{\"role\": \"system\"}` entry placed first in the `messages` array","Inside the `tools` array, so that they apply whenever a tool is invoked","Appended to the front of the first user message, ahead of the actual question","In the top-level `system` field of the request"],c:[3],e:"The top-level `system` field carries instructions that apply from the start; a system-role message is not permitted as the first entry in `messages`."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"On Claude Opus 5, what is true about placing a `{\"role\": \"system\"}` message inside the `messages` array?",o:["It is rejected outright, since system content belongs only in the top-level field","It is allowed anywhere in the array, including as the very first entry","It is allowed only as the final entry, after every user and assistant turn","It is allowed after a user turn, but it cannot be the first entry in the array"],c:[3],e:"Mid-conversation system messages are supported on Opus 5 (and Fable 5 and Opus 4.8) after a user turn, carry the same authority as the top-level field, and don't invalidate the cached prefix before them — but they cannot open the array."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"What must every Messages API request supply at minimum to be valid?",o:["A `tools` array and an explicit `system` prompt","A model identifier, a `messages` array, and a `max_tokens` value","At least one image content block, even for text-only tasks","A model identifier and a `messages` array, with `max_tokens` defaulting automatically"],c:[1],e:"Those three fields are the baseline every request needs; tools, system, and caching are all optional additions."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"After the model returns a `tool_use` content block, what does the calling application need to do before the conversation can continue?",o:["Switch the request into streaming mode before anything else can happen","Execute the requested tool and send its result back as a `tool_result` block in a subsequent request","Immediately show the raw `tool_use` block to the end user as the final answer","Discard the block and re-prompt the model from the beginning, since a tool request indicates that the turn has failed"],c:[1],e:"Tool use is a round trip: the app runs the tool, then reports the outcome back so the model can continue."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A `tool_result` block is linked back to the model's request by:",o:["Referencing the `id` of the specific `tool_use` block it is answering","Including a hash of the entire prior conversation history","Being sent within the same streaming event as the original tool_use block","Matching on the exact text of the tool's name alone, with no id involved"],c:[0],e:"The `id` field is what ties a tool_use request to its corresponding tool_result response."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"The Messages API is stateless between calls. What does this mean for a multi-turn agentic loop?",o:["Your application must resend the relevant prior turns on each subsequent call","Statelessness only affects vision requests, not text or tool-use turns","Nothing needs to be resent, since the API automatically retains full history server-side by default","Only the original system prompt needs to be resent, with all message history dropped"],c:[0],e:"Because there's no server-side session state by default, the calling application owns reconstructing the relevant history each call."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A chat UI needs generated text to visibly appear word-by-word as it's produced. Which mechanism enables this?",o:["Streaming, which delivers the response as a sequence of incremental events","The Message Batches API, which is asynchronous and not meant for live UI updates","Prompt caching, which only affects repeated prefixes, not delivery timing","Vision content blocks, which are unrelated to how text is delivered"],c:[0],e:"Streaming is specifically the mechanism for incremental delivery of output as it's generated."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A `content_block_delta` event received during a streamed response represents:",o:["An incremental piece of the response as it's generated","A tool_result being returned to the model","A notification that the prompt cache was invalidated","The entire finished message, sent as one single event"],c:[0],e:"Delta events are the incremental chunks that streaming sends as generation progresses."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An integration must validate the model's entire JSON response as one complete object before doing anything with it. What does this suggest about streaming?",o:["Streaming likely isn't worth the added complexity here","Streaming is mandatory in this case regardless of the validation requirement","Streaming would make validating the complete object easier, not harder","This applies mainly when tool use is disabled, since tool results arrive as discrete blocks"],c:[0],e:"If you need the whole object before acting, incremental delivery doesn't help and just adds reassembly work."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Which content type lets Claude read the contents of a screenshot or photograph provided in a request?",o:["A cache_control block marked as ephemeral","A vision (image) content block within the message","An extended thinking block, which only carries the model's own reasoning text","A `tool_result` block, regardless of what generated the image"],c:[1],e:"Image understanding is handled via image content blocks placed directly in the message content."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A finance workflow needs to read scanned invoice images and return structured JSON totals. Which combination of capabilities is directly relevant?",o:["The Message Batches API specifically, with no need for vision at all","Prompt caching of the invoice template, combined with a higher effort level per request","Vision content blocks plus a structured-output request with downstream validation","Prompt caching alone, since the invoices are the same length each time"],c:[2],e:"Reading the scanned images requires vision; reliably getting totals out requires requesting and then validating structured output."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An overnight job must process 50,000 records with no requirement for an immediate response, and cost efficiency matters most. Which approach fits best?",o:["The Message Batches API, which processes large volumes asynchronously at half price","Synchronous calls issued one after another in a tight loop","Extended thinking enabled on every single record regardless of complexity","Streaming every response to a user interface that no one is watching overnight"],c:[0],e:"High volume, latency tolerance, and cost sensitivity together are exactly the Batch API's design target."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A single Message Batch submission is limited to:",o:["100,000 requests or 256 MB of total size, whichever is reached first","50,000 requests or 128 MB of total size, whichever is reached first","1,000 requests per batch, with no separate limit on total payload size","Unlimited requests, bounded only by the 24-hour processing window"],c:[0],e:"The documented cap is 100,000 requests or 256 MB per batch, whichever limit is hit first."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"When retrieving results from a completed Message Batch, an application should match each result back to its original request using:",o:["The timestamp on each result, compared against the request's submission time","The position of each result in the results file, which mirrors submission order","The `message.id` on each result, matched against the id returned at submission","The `custom_id` that was supplied with that request at submission time"],c:[3],e:"Results can come back in any order, so `custom_id` is the only dependable way to correlate them with their requests."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A request sets `stream: true` inside a batch of requests submitted to the Message Batches API. What happens?",o:["It's accepted, and each batched request streams independently in real time","It automatically converts the entire batch into synchronous calls","It's silently ignored with no effect on the request at all","It's rejected — streaming isn't supported for batch requests"],c:[3],e:"Certain parameters, including `stream: true`, are explicitly incompatible with the asynchronous nature of batch processing and return a validation error."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Batch results remain available for download after a batch completes for approximately:",o:["Indefinitely, with no expiration on stored results","29 days from batch creation, after which results are no longer downloadable","24 hours, after which the batch and all of its results are permanently deleted","24 hours, matching the window in which the batch itself has to finish processing"],c:[1],e:"Batch results stay downloadable for 29 days after creation; the processing window itself is a separate, shorter figure."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A system prompt is long and unchanging, while each request appends a short, unique user question. What reduces cost and latency here?",o:["Switching to the cheapest available model tier regardless of the task's difficulty","Trimming the unique question down, since it is the part that changes on every call","Marking the stable system prompt as cacheable so its tokens get reused, not reprocessed","Moving the system prompt to the end of the request, after the user's question"],c:[2],e:"A large, stable prefix is exactly what caching targets. Moving it after the varying question would destroy the shared prefix entirely."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"By default, how long does a prompt cache entry remain valid if it isn't reused?",o:["5 minutes, measured from the start of the request, refreshed on each reuse","1 hour, which is the only cache lifetime the platform offers for stored content","24 hours by default, matching the Batch API's processing window","30 seconds, with no way to extend it under any circumstance"],c:[0],e:"The default cache lifetime is 5 minutes from the start of the triggering request, refreshed on each subsequent hit."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A background agent session sometimes goes longer than 5 minutes between calls that share a cached prefix, and the team wants to avoid repeated cache-write costs. What's the direct fix?",o:["Switch the session to the Batch API, which doesn't support prompt caching at all","Re-send a lightweight request every few minutes purely to keep the existing cache entry warm and alive","Add a second cache breakpoint further along the prompt so a later write can be picked up","Use the 1-hour cache duration option instead of the 5-minute default, at its higher per-write price"],c:[3],e:"The 1-hour TTL exists specifically for reuse gaps too long for the 5-minute default but still valuable to keep warm."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Prompt caching treats a request's content in which hierarchy?",o:["System only, with tool definitions and message history excluded from the cached prefix","Messages, then system, then tools, with no dependency between levels","Messages first, then system and tools, so that recent turns can be cached independently","Tools, then system, then messages, with earlier changes invalidating what follows"],c:[3],e:"Caching follows a tools → system → messages hierarchy, and a change at one level invalidates it and every level that follows."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An application can define at most how many explicit cache breakpoints in a single request?",o:["4","Unlimited","1","10"],c:[0],e:"Explicit `cache_control` breakpoints are capped at 4 per request, and automatic caching consumes one of those slots."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team caches a system prompt that's only 300 tokens long and never sees a cache hit register in the usage fields. What's the most likely explanation?",o:["The prompt falls below the minimum cacheable token threshold for that model","Short prompts are rejected with a validation error rather than processed without caching","The `cache_control` field only applies to image content, never to text","Caching silently caps out at exactly 500 tokens for every model with no exceptions"],c:[0],e:"Minimum cacheable lengths vary by model, and prompts under that threshold are simply processed uncached with no error."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A large, reused reference document is cached, and the same request is also submitted through the Message Batches API for cost reasons. Is this combination valid?",o:["Yes — caching and batching address different cost levers and can be combined","No — enabling the Batch API automatically strips any cache_control fields from the request","No — caching and batching are mutually exclusive features that cannot appear together","Yes, but only if vision content is also present in the request"],c:[0],e:"The two mechanisms optimize different things and are designed to stack, including inside batch requests."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team adds per-request dynamic effort tuning to an agent loop and notices their message-level cache hit rate drops sharply. Why?",o:["Changing the effort value between requests invalidates the message-level cache","Dynamic effort routes each request to a different model snapshot, missing the cache","Effort changes invalidate the tool definitions cache, leaving message entries intact","The cache TTL is reset to zero whenever any request parameter is altered"],c:[0],e:"Effort is rendered into the prompt, so varying it between requests always invalidates message-level cache entries."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A production incident traces back to a model alias silently changing behavior after Anthropic shipped an update. What practice would have prevented this?",o:["Enabling extended thinking on every request","Switching every call to go through the Batch API","Pinning the integration to a dated model snapshot instead of a rolling alias","Raising max_tokens across the board so responses have more room to stabilize between releases"],c:[2],e:"Dated, pinned snapshots don't shift behavior the way an alias pointing at 'whatever's current' can."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team wants to check how many tokens a long request will consume before actually sending it for generation. What supports this?",o:["Submitting the request with `max_tokens` set to zero and reading the response","The token counting endpoint, which returns a count without running generation","The usage figures on the prior response, which predict the next request's size","Dividing the character count by four, which the API treats as authoritative"],c:[1],e:"Token counting exists precisely so you can size a request against context limits and cost before committing to a generation call."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An integration needs the model's response to conform to a specific JSON shape that downstream code can rely on. Which capability is designed for this?",o:["Structured outputs, which constrain the response to a supplied output format","Prompt caching, which stores a repeated prefix but leaves output shape untouched","Streaming, which changes delivery timing rather than the structure produced","The Files API, which handles uploads rather than the shape of a response"],c:[0],e:"Structured outputs constrain the generated response to a defined format; the other three affect cost, inputs, or delivery instead."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A backend integration starts receiving HTTP 429 responses during a traffic spike. What is the appropriate handling?",o:["Retry immediately in a tight loop until one of the attempts eventually succeeds","Queue the excess requests locally and then submit them all at once as soon as the spike subsides","Treat it as a permanent failure and stop sending requests for the rest of the day","Back off and retry with increasing delays, respecting any retry timing the response supplies"],c:[3],e:"429 signals rate limiting, which calls for backoff and retry — not immediate hammering, permanent surrender, or a model swap."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A tool's `input_schema` (JSON Schema) exists to:",o:["Authenticate the request against the Anthropic API","Style how the tool's results are displayed in a chat interface","Control which prompt-cache breakpoint the tool's definition uses","Define the structure and types of arguments the model should supply"],c:[3],e:"The schema shapes what arguments the model is expected to produce for a given tool call."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An application needs Claude to read a multi-page PDF contract and answer questions about its clauses. How is the PDF supplied?",o:["As a top-level parameter that sits outside the messages array entirely","By converting every page to plain text first, since PDFs cannot be sent","As a document content block within the message, alongside the user's question","As a tool definition entry, with the file path given in its input schema"],c:[2],e:"PDFs are passed as document content blocks in the message, the same structural pattern images follow."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A feature parses the model's text output as JSON downstream. What should the integration code do regardless of how the JSON was requested?",o:["Disable all error handling around parsing to reduce latency","Defensively validate and handle malformed or unexpected output","Only accept the output if it also contains a vision content block","Assume the response matches the requested schema, since the request specified the format"],c:[1],e:"Even with a structured-output request, downstream code should still validate rather than assume perfect compliance."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"What does `max_tokens` govern in a Messages API request?",o:["The combined total of input and output tokens permitted for the request","The maximum number of input tokens the request is allowed to contain","The number of tool calls the model is permitted to make in one turn","The maximum number of tokens the model is allowed to generate in its response"],c:[3],e:"max_tokens caps generated output length; it doesn't bound the input, cache TTL, or tool-call count directly."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A summarization feature sets `max_tokens` well below what the task typically needs. What is the most likely result?",o:["The excess is silently billed as though the full budget had been produced","The model plans a shorter summary up front so that the whole answer fits the smaller budget","Responses get cut off mid-generation, coming back with a `max_tokens` stop reason","The model automatically compresses its answer to fit the smaller budget"],c:[2],e:"max_tokens is a hard ceiling on generated output; setting it too low truncates the response rather than prompting the model to self-compress."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Why is exact-match string comparison a poor default assertion for testing an LLM feature's output?",o:["Because the API guarantees byte-identical output on every call, making the assertion redundant","Because exact-match assertions only ever fail on vision-based tasks","Because output quality can only be judged by a human reviewer reading the actual response","Sampling-driven non-determinism means identical prompts can validly produce different wordings"],c:[3],e:"Expected output variability makes exact-match a fragile choice; tests should target acceptable properties instead."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A CI pipeline for an LLM feature runs only traditional unit tests on the surrounding code. What's missing?",o:["Automated eval checks assessing the quality and correctness of the model's actual outputs","Nothing — traditional unit tests are sufficient for any LLM-integrated feature","A mandatory switch to the Batch API before any test can run","A load test confirming that the feature holds up under peak production traffic volumes"],c:[0],e:"LLM output quality needs its own eval-style checks in CI, layered on top of ordinary unit testing of the surrounding code."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A code review finds a mobile app's API key embedded directly in the client-side bundle. This is primarily a failure of:",o:["Secure secret management — client-side code isn't a safe place for API credentials","Model version pinning discipline, which concerns behavior stability, not secrets","Streaming protocol conventions, which don't govern secret handling","Prompt caching configuration, which is unrelated to how secrets are stored"],c:[0],e:"Hardcoded client-side secrets are a textbook secret-management failure, independent of any LLM-specific concern."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Code review for an LLM-integration pull request should specifically add checks for, beyond ordinary code quality:",o:["Whether the chosen model tier is documented in a comment at each call site","Whether every prompt string is centralized in one module for easier reading","Whether retry logic wraps every call so transient failures never reach a user","Prompt injection exposure, tool-call error handling, and secret management"],c:[3],e:"The three named risks are LLM-specific and security-relevant; the others are reasonable style preferences that don't address those risks."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Refactoring a tangled prompt-assembly function into smaller, named pieces primarily improves:",o:["Automatic enablement of prompt caching with no other changes needed","Maintainability and testability as the surrounding requirements continue to evolve","The accuracy of the model's responses, since a cleaner prompt is inherently easier to follow","The model's inherent reasoning capability on the resulting prompts"],c:[1],e:"Refactoring is a code-health investment; it doesn't directly change model behavior or automatically trigger caching."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Which reflects sound REST API design applied to the service layer wrapping a Claude integration?",o:["Randomizing response shapes between calls to discourage client-side caching","Endpoint names copied directly from internal database table names with no consistency","Resource-oriented endpoints, predictable status codes, and a clear versioning strategy","Avoiding the use of standard HTTP status codes wherever possible"],c:[2],e:"Standard REST discipline — clear resources, predictable status codes, versioning — still applies to the layer around your Claude calls."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Why are async/await patterns generally recommended for LLM API calls inside a concurrent web backend?",o:["Because async lets a single request be split across several cores, shortening total wall time","Because LLM calls are CPU-bound and async is the only way to use multiple cores","LLM calls are I/O-bound, and blocking synchronously on them wastes resources under load","Because it removes the need for any error handling around the call"],c:[2],e:"The benefit is specifically about not blocking on network I/O while waiting for a response, which matters most under concurrency."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A stakeholder asks for an LLM feature that will 'make our support better,' with nothing more specific. What should happen before implementation choices are made?",o:["The vague goal should be decomposed into concrete, testable use cases and success criteria","Implementation should start immediately with the most capable available model tier","The most capable available model tier should be selected before scoping the work any further","The team should ship a first version quickly and let production usage define the requirements"],c:[0],e:"Turning ambiguous asks into testable criteria is foundational groundwork that should precede technical choices like model tier."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Why does requirements gathering for an LLM feature often take more deliberate effort than for traditional deterministic software?",o:["Because the output format has to be fixed before launch and cannot change later","Because the context window imposes size limits that traditional software does not have","Because stakeholders have to specify the model tier and effort level up front","Success criteria for open-ended, probabilistic output are harder to pin down precisely"],c:[3],e:"Defining what a 'good' answer looks like is the genuinely harder part; context limits and configuration choices are implementation details settled later."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An iterative build-measure-learn cycle applied to an LLM feature typically starts with:",o:["Deferring measurement until the feature is close to its final planned release state","Shipping a minimal version and observing real failure patterns first","A single, fully polished release with no further changes planned afterward","Finalizing model tier only after every other design decision is locked in"],c:[1],e:"Starting minimal and learning from real observed failures is the core of the iterative loop, rather than over-building upfront."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Which practice most directly reduces the impact of a bad model response actually reaching a user?",o:["Raising the effort level so the model reasons more carefully before responding","Adding more few-shot examples so the model matches the desired format closely","Logging every response so the support team can review problems afterwards","Validating or guarding the output before it is shown or acted on"],c:[3],e:"The others reduce how often a bad response is produced or help you find one later; only a guard between generation and delivery limits the impact of one that does occur."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A high-traffic, cost-sensitive customer-facing feature needs predictable spend at scale. Which combination best addresses this?",o:["Raising max_tokens so that fewer follow-up requests are needed to complete a single answer","Vision content blocks added to every request regardless of whether images are involved","The most capable tier for every call, with caching and batching both disabled","A right-sized model tier, prompt caching for shared context, and batching for non-real-time work"],c:[3],e:"Layering tier-matching, caching, and batching together is the standard cost-optimization stack for high-traffic features."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A product requirement demands a single response within 300ms. Which mechanism is ruled out by that constraint?",o:["Synchronous calls using a fast model tier, which can plausibly meet tight latency budgets","Prompt caching, since writing a brand-new cache entry adds noticeable latency to the very first call in a sequence","The Message Batches API, since it processes asynchronously and cannot guarantee an immediate response","Tool use in general, which has no inherent effect on request latency by itself"],c:[2],e:"Batch processing is fundamentally asynchronous and unsuited to a hard, immediate single-response latency requirement."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Anthropic's Files API is most useful for:",o:["Converting uploaded documents into embeddings for similarity search","Archiving past responses so they can be retrieved again by id later","Uploading a file once and referencing it across multiple subsequent requests","Storing conversation history server-side so it need not be resent each turn"],c:[2],e:"The Files API avoids re-transmitting the same file on every request; it is not a conversation store, an embedding service, or a response archive."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A RAG application wants each generated claim to point back to the specific source passage it came from. Which capability is designed for this?",o:["The Message Batches API, which affects processing mode, not sourcing","Citations, which attach source attribution to generated content","Prompt caching, which affects cost and latency but not attribution","Extended thinking, which exposes reasoning but not source attribution"],c:[1],e:"Citations are the feature purpose-built for natural, per-claim source attribution in retrieval-style applications."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Structured output requests (asking the model to conform to a defined output format) reduce, but don't eliminate, the need for:",o:["Defensive parsing and validation of the response actually received","Prompt caching, which structured output requests are incompatible with","Tool use, which is unrelated to structured output requests","Model selection, since a structured request makes the choice of tier largely irrelevant"],c:[0],e:"Requesting structure improves the odds of compliant output, but the integration should still verify what actually came back."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"`tool_choice` in a Messages API request controls:",o:["Whether and how the model is permitted or required to use tools on that turn","How long the prompt cache entry for that request remains valid","Which of the defined tools is executed first when several are requested at once","Whether the response is streamed or returned synchronously"],c:[0],e:"tool_choice governs the model's tool-use behavior for the turn, separate from model tier, streaming, or caching."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A single model turn returns multiple `tool_use` blocks requesting several different tool calls at once. This is best described as:",o:["An error state, since a turn may contain at most one tool request at a time","A retry, where the model repeats one call until an attempt eventually succeeds","Programmatic tool calling, where the model writes code that invokes the tools","Parallel tool use, where the model requests more than one call in a single turn"],c:[3],e:"Requesting several tools in one turn is the supported parallel tool use pattern, distinct from programmatic tool calling and from retrying a single call."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A streamed response arrives as a sequence of events. What must the application do to obtain the complete final text?",o:["Read the final event alone, since it repeats the entire message in full","Accumulate the incremental delta events in order as they arrive","Sort the events by length, since streaming makes no ordering guarantees","Reissue the same request without streaming to obtain the whole response"],c:[1],e:"Streaming delivers ordered incremental pieces that the client assembles; no single event carries the whole message."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A response reports `cache_read_input_tokens`, `cache_creation_input_tokens`, and `input_tokens`. How is total input calculated?",o:["By reading `input_tokens` alone, which already totals everything that was sent","By adding the two cache figures, since ordinary input is billed separately","By taking the largest of the three, since the fields overlap one another","By adding all three together, since each covers a different portion of the prompt"],c:[3],e:"`input_tokens` counts only tokens after the last cache breakpoint, so the three fields sum to the true total input."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"The extended/adaptive thinking content block, when present in a response, is exposed:",o:["As its own distinct content block, separate from the final answer text","Only when the response is delivered synchronously rather than streamed","Only via a completely separate API endpoint requiring a second request","Merged into the final answer text, so the reasoning and the answer arrive as one block"],c:[0],e:"Thinking content comes back as its own block type alongside, but distinct from, the final answer."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Some requests in a submitted batch are still unprocessed when the batch reaches its 24-hour limit. What happens to them?",o:["They are marked expired and are not billed, while completed requests still return results","They are automatically resubmitted as a fresh batch, which starts its own 24-hour window","They continue processing past the limit until every request eventually finishes","They are billed in full despite never having been sent to the model at all"],c:[0],e:"Batches expire at 24 hours; unprocessed requests come back as expired and unbilled, and already-finished results remain available."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A response comes back with `stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"` after a server tool (like web search) was used mid-turn. What does this indicate?",o:["The request failed outright and must be resubmitted from scratch","The model exceeded its max_tokens budget, so the response was truncated partway through","The turn hasn't finished — continue it with the paused content in a follow-up request","The conversation has permanently ended and no further turns are possible"],c:[2],e:"`pause_turn` signals an in-progress server-side turn that needs to be continued, not a failure or a hard stop."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Reviewing the `stop_reason` field on a response is useful because:",o:["It is only populated when the request used vision content","It reports the total number of tokens consumed, which is the figure used for cost attribution","It reveals the specific reason generation stopped, which should drive different handling","It replaces the need to check whether a tool_use block is present"],c:[2],e:"Different stop reasons call for different follow-up logic, so checking this field is a core part of building a correct integration."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team notices their prompt-cache read tokens don't count against their organization's rate limit the way uncached input tokens do. What's the practical implication?",o:["Cached requests are exempt from billing as well, since the tokens were already paid for once","This behavior only applies to requests submitted via the Batch API","A high cache-hit rate can meaningfully improve effective rate-limit headroom","Cache reads instead count double against the rate limit as a tradeoff"],c:[2],e:"Because cache hits aren't deducted from rate limits, effective caching helps with both cost and available request throughput."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A regulated industry's compliance team asks how request and response content is retained by default. What's the relevant integration consideration?",o:["Nothing — no Claude API feature has any data retention behavior worth reviewing","Understanding the platform's data retention and ZDR options, since eligibility varies by feature","ZDR eligibility is identical and automatic across every single API feature with no review needed","Retention only matters for requests that use vision content blocks"],c:[1],e:"Retention and ZDR eligibility genuinely differ by feature, so a compliance-sensitive integration needs to actually check, not assume."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A team deciding between a conversational chat UI and a fully autonomous backend agent for the same task should weigh primarily:",o:["How much visibility and control the situation actually calls for","The model tier chosen, which should be decided independently of interface design","Whether tools are involved at all, since that alone should decide the interface","Which option is cheaper to build regardless of how well it fits the task"],c:[0],e:"Interface choice should follow the task's actual need for human visibility and control, not be decided on unrelated grounds."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"A fully autonomous backend agent with no human review step is most defensible when:",o:["The task is high-risk and difficult to verify once it's already been taken","The task is well-bounded, relatively low-risk, and its outcomes are easy to verify after the fact","The task is high-stakes enough that any mistake would be expensive, which makes hands-off speed the priority","Users have explicitly asked for maximum visibility into every step taken"],c:[1],e:"Full autonomy fits situations where mistakes are cheap to catch and correct, not high-stakes or hard-to-verify ones."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"Version control hygiene for an LLM-integrated codebase should include:",o:["Small, reviewable commits alongside pinned model and dependency versions","Reviewing each change only after it has already been deployed to production users","API keys committed directly to the repository for convenience","One large commit at the end of each development cycle"],c:[0],e:"Small commits and pinned versions support traceability and stable, reviewable behavior over time."},
{d:"D2",t:"s",q:"An agent's tool call succeeds externally, but the network response is lost and the loop retries it. What design property prevents duplicate side effects?",o:["Making the underlying operation idempotent, so a repeat call has no additional effect","Raising the request timeout, which lengthens the wait before a retry occurs","Choosing a faster model tier, which narrows the window for a lost response","Lowering the effort level, so the agent makes fewer tool calls and retries less often overall"],c:[0],e:"Idempotency is what makes a retried operation safe; timeouts, streaming, and model speed narrow the window but don't remove the risk."},
{d:"D2",t:"m",q:"Which two are parameters or features explicitly unsupported inside a Message Batches API request? (Select 2)",o:["System prompts via the top-level `system` parameter","`stream: true`","Fast mode's `speed` parameter","Tool definitions in the `tools` array"],c:[1,2],e:"Streaming and fast mode's speed setting are both explicitly incompatible with asynchronous batch processing; tools and system prompts work fine in batches."},
{d:"D2",t:"m",q:"Which three of these are true about combining prompt caching with the Message Batches API on the same workload? (Select 3)",o:["Cost efficiency matters more than immediate turnaround for this workload","A large, stable prefix reused across many of the batched requests supports caching","Cache hits inside a batch are provided on a best-effort basis, not a guarantee","The workload requires a guaranteed sub-second response for each individual request"],c:[0,1,2],e:"A shared stable prefix, a cost-over-latency priority, and best-effort (not guaranteed) cache hits are all accurate for batched caching; a hard sub-second guarantee conflicts with how batching works."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A feature classifies thousands of short product reviews per minute as positive or negative, with no need for nuanced judgment. Which model choice fits best?",o:["A mid-tier model such as Claude Sonnet 5, chosen to leave headroom for occasional ambiguous reviews","Whichever model has the largest context window, regardless of task needs","The fastest, cheapest tier suited to simple, high-volume classification, such as Claude Haiku 4.5","The most capable tier available, regardless of the task's actual simplicity"],c:[2],e:"Simple, high-volume classification is the textbook case for the fastest, cheapest tier rather than over-provisioning capability."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team needs the single most capable widely released Claude model for an extremely difficult analysis task, accepting the highest cost. Which should they choose?",o:["Claude Fable 5","Claude Haiku 4.5","Claude Sonnet 5","Claude Opus 5"],c:[0],e:"Anthropic positions Fable 5 as its most capable widely released model; Opus 5 is the recommended default for agentic coding and enterprise work rather than the top capability tier."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A token, in the context of how Claude processes text, is best understood as:",o:["One entire API request, regardless of its length","One tool call made during a turn","A sub-word unit of text used for model processing","Exactly one character of input or output, with no exceptions"],c:[2],e:"Tokens are sub-word units; they don't map cleanly onto a fixed character or word count."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A model's context window represents:",o:["The number of tool definitions permitted in a single request","Only the number of tokens the model may generate in its response","The combined input-plus-output token budget available for a single request","Only the input tokens a request may contain, with output governed separately"],c:[2],e:"The context window is a shared ceiling spanning both what goes into and comes out of one request."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Why can two calls sent with identical prompts return meaningfully different wording in their responses?",o:["Vision inputs are randomized between identical calls by design","The model re-reads portions of its training data at request time, which varies between calls","Sampling-driven variability is an inherent, expected part of how generation works","This indicates a bug that should be reported for every occurrence"],c:[2],e:"Non-determinism from sampling is expected behavior, distinct from caching or vision-related effects."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Claude Haiku 4.5 differs from Claude Fable 5, Claude Opus 5, and Claude Sonnet 5 in its thinking behavior in that Haiku 4.5:",o:["Uses adaptive thinking exactly the same way the other three do","Uses a toggleable extended-thinking mode instead of always-adaptive thinking","Thinks at a fixed depth on every request, with no per-request control over how much","Is the only one of the four with no thinking capability of any kind"],c:[1],e:"Haiku 4.5 supports classic extended thinking as an on/off toggle, while Fable 5, Opus 5, and Sonnet 5 use adaptive thinking that the model itself controls."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"The `effort` parameter is best described as:",o:["A fixed, unchangeable property of a given model that cannot be set per request","A per-request lever trading off response thoroughness against token efficiency","A billing tier selection rather than a behavioral control","A setting that only affects vision-based requests"],c:[1],e:"Effort is a request-level control over token spend across the whole response, tool calls included, not a static model property."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which named effort level does the API use by default when the `effort` parameter is omitted from a request?",o:["There is no default; omitting `effort` removes the parameter's influence","`medium`, chosen as a balance between cost and quality unless overridden","`high` — leaving it unset behaves exactly like setting it explicitly","`xhigh` on models that support it, falling back to `high` on those that don't"],c:[2],e:"The documented default is high, and setting `effort` to \"high\" produces exactly the same behavior as omitting it."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which of these correctly orders the named effort levels from most conservative about token spend to least constrained?",o:["high, medium, xhigh, low, max","max, xhigh, high, medium, low","medium, low, high, max, xhigh","low, medium, high, xhigh, max"],c:[3],e:"The five effort levels run low, medium, high (default), xhigh, then max, from most token-conservative to least constrained."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"The `xhigh` effort level is available on a narrower set of models than `max`. Which of these does NOT support `xhigh`?",o:["Claude Sonnet 5","Claude Opus 4.6","Claude Fable 5","Claude Opus 5"],c:[1],e:"xhigh is supported on Fable 5, Opus 5, Opus 4.8, Opus 4.7, and Sonnet 5, but Opus 4.6 supports only up to max, not xhigh."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which of Anthropic's current named models does the `effort` parameter NOT apply to?",o:["Claude Opus 5","Claude Haiku 4.5","Claude Fable 5","Claude Sonnet 5"],c:[1],e:"The effort parameter's documented compatibility covers Fable 5, Opus 4.5 and later, and Sonnet 4.6/5 — Haiku 4.5 isn't among the supported models."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Fast mode for Opus is best described as:",o:["A smaller distilled variant of Opus that trades away some quality in exchange for lower latency","The same Opus model and quality, running at roughly 2.5x the speed for a higher per-token cost","A free optimization with no cost tradeoff versus standard-speed Opus","An entirely different, smaller model swapped in behind the scenes for speed"],c:[1],e:"Fast mode changes the API configuration for latency, not the model or its quality — and it costs more per token, not less."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Which statement about Fast mode's model availability is accurate?",o:["It runs on every current model, but only through the Console API","It runs on Opus, and is not offered on Sonnet, Haiku, or Fable","It runs on Sonnet and Haiku, where latency matters most at high volume","It runs on Opus and Fable, the two highest-capability model families"],c:[1],e:"Fast mode is an Opus-only configuration; it is not available on Sonnet, Haiku, or Fable."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"How do Fast mode and a lower effort level differ as ways to get a faster response from Opus?",o:["Fast mode reduces quality while lower effort increases cost, the reverse of their actual behavior","They are two names for exactly the same underlying mechanism","Fast mode keeps quality constant and lowers latency for a higher cost; lower effort trades quality for speed","Fast mode reduces the number of thinking tokens spent, while a lower effort level routes the request to a physically faster cluster"],c:[2],e:"The two levers work differently: one holds quality constant and raises cost for speed, the other trades depth of reasoning for speed and cost savings."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team wants maximum speed on a straightforward task without needing full reasoning depth. What combination fits Anthropic's documented guidance?",o:["Enable Fast mode together with the max effort level for best results","Leave effort at its default, since Fast mode already accounts for the reasoning depth","Use the Batch API together with Fast mode for the lowest possible latency","Enable Fast mode together with a lower effort level"],c:[3],e:"Fast mode and a lower effort level are designed to be combined for maximum speed on tasks that don't need deep reasoning."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Why doesn't Fast mode apply to requests submitted through the Message Batches API?",o:["Fast mode requires vision content blocks to be present in the request","Batch requests already run at maximum speed, so the setting would have nothing further to improve","Fast mode is too expensive to ever be offered on any high-volume workload","Fast mode optimizes synchronous latency, and batch processing has no live latency to optimize"],c:[3],e:"Fast mode's entire purpose is reducing perceived latency on a live request, which doesn't map onto asynchronous batch processing."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A cost review shows a feature using cache write tokens far more than cache read tokens, at a notably higher per-token price than base input. What does this reflect about caching's pricing structure?",o:["Both cache writes and cache reads are billed at the same discounted rate below base input pricing","Cache reads cost more than cache writes, the reverse of their actual pricing","Writing a new cache entry costs more per token than base input; reading one costs far less","Cache writes and cache reads are priced the same, both below the base input token rate"],c:[2],e:"Cache writes carry a premium over base input price, while cache reads are priced well below it — the pricing intentionally rewards reuse over first-time writes."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Roughly how much cheaper is the Message Batches API compared to standard synchronous pricing, for the same model and volume?",o:["About 25% cheaper, applied to input tokens only","About 10% cheaper, matching the prompt-cache read discount","About 75% cheaper, but only on the highest-capability models","About 50% cheaper, applied to both input and output tokens"],c:[3],e:"Batch usage is charged at 50% of standard pricing across the board, not a partial or model-specific discount."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Comparing current model context windows, which is true?",o:["Context window size is unrelated to which model you're using","All current models share exactly the same context window size","Every current model shares a 200k-token window, with larger inputs handled by chunking","Haiku 4.5's context window (200k tokens) is smaller than the others' 1M tokens"],c:[3],e:"Haiku 4.5 tops out at 200k tokens while the other three current-generation models support a 1M-token context window."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Ranking current named models from lowest to highest per-token output price, which order is correct?",o:["Sonnet 5, then Haiku 4.5, then Fable 5, then Opus 5","Fable 5, then Opus 5, then Sonnet 5, then Haiku 4.5","Haiku 4.5, then Sonnet 5, then Opus 5, then Fable 5","All four are priced identically per output token"],c:[2],e:"Pricing scales from Haiku 4.5 as the cheapest tier up through Sonnet 5, Opus 5, and Fable 5 as the most expensive."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A cost-sensitive feature has moderate complexity and needs reasonably fast responses, without an obvious need for the top tier's full capability. What's a sound default starting point?",o:["The most capable tier available, chosen without evaluating whether it's actually needed","A balanced mid-tier model like Claude Sonnet 5, evaluated before assuming a higher tier","A randomly selected tier on each request to average out cost","The cheapest tier available, chosen without evaluating whether it holds quality"],c:[1],e:"A balanced mid-tier model is a reasonable default for moderate-complexity, production-scale work, subject to evaluation."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A cost audit finds the most capable tier being used for simple yes/no classification. What's the likely fix?",o:["Keep the tier but lower the effort level, so the same model spends fewer tokens per classification","Right-size the tier down to a faster, cheaper model suited to the task's actual complexity","Add a higher effort level on top of the current tier to further improve results","Switch to the Batch API exclusively, without reconsidering the tier itself"],c:[1],e:"Over-provisioned tier for a simple task is fixed by right-sizing down, not by adding more capability or effort on top."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"What's the main risk of defaulting to the cheapest available model tier for every task in an application, regardless of complexity?",o:["Higher overall spend, since cheaper tiers need more retries to reach an acceptable answer","No risk at all, since cost is the only factor that ever matters","Degraded quality or accuracy on the subset of tasks that genuinely need deeper reasoning","Faster responses on most tasks, with the tradeoff limited to slightly longer outputs"],c:[2],e:"Under-provisioning tier for genuinely hard tasks costs quality, even though it looks efficient for the easy ones."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Why is model tier typically chosen per feature or per task, rather than once for an entire application?",o:["Because each API key is scoped to a single model, which forces a separate key for each feature","Different features in the same app can have very different complexity and cost needs","Because the optimal tier tends to converge once a feature reaches production traffic","Because an API account is only permitted to use one model at a time"],c:[1],e:"Task diversity within one app is exactly why tier decisions are usually made per task rather than app-wide."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team's evals show a mid-tier model performing inconsistently on a subset of unusually hard cases within an otherwise simple workload. What's a reasonable model-selection response?",o:["Move the entire workload to the most expensive tier to be safe","Route the harder subset to a higher tier, keeping the rest on the cheaper one","Switch the entire workload to the cheapest available tier instead","Ignore the inconsistency, since evals are not meant to inform tier choice"],c:[1],e:"Complexity-based routing lets you preserve quality where it's actually needed without over-paying across the whole workload."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Comparing minimum cacheable prompt lengths, Claude Haiku 4.5 requires a prompt of at least how many tokens before caching applies, versus Claude Opus 5's much lower threshold?",o:["1 token for both, since caching has no minimum length requirement","4,096 tokens for Opus 5, versus 512 tokens for Haiku 4.5, the reverse of the actual thresholds","4,096 tokens for Haiku 4.5, versus 512 tokens for Opus 5","512 tokens for both models, with no difference between them"],c:[2],e:"Minimum cacheable length varies by model, and Haiku 4.5's threshold is notably higher than Opus 5's — a real factor when deciding whether caching will actually help a given Haiku workload."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"When effort is applied to a request that includes tool use, lower effort levels tend to:",o:["Increase the total number of tool calls made compared to higher effort","Increase the number of tool calls made, since less reasoning per call means more calls are needed","Combine multiple operations into fewer tool calls and proceed with less explanatory preamble","Restrict the model to a single tool call per turn, regardless of how many are defined"],c:[2],e:"Lower effort tends to economize on tool calls and explanation, while higher effort tends to make more calls with more detailed explanation."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"On Claude Opus 5, what happens if a request sets `thinking: {\"type\": \"disabled\"}` together with `xhigh` or `max` effort?",o:["The request succeeds, but the thinking blocks are omitted from the response","The request is rejected with a 400 error, since thinking can't be disabled at those levels","The request is retried once automatically, with the thinking parameter removed","The request succeeds and the effort level is automatically stepped down to high"],c:[1],e:"Opus 5 treats this combination as invalid and returns a 400 rather than silently adjusting effort or suppressing output."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"A team is unsure upfront how complex a new feature's real-world inputs will turn out to be. What's a reasonable model-selection strategy?",o:["Begin with the cheapest tier and upgrade only after users report quality problems in production","Start with the most capable tier available, then step down once the workload is understood","Refuse to build the feature until complexity is fully known in advance","Start with a mid-tier model and escalate only if evaluation shows it's insufficient"],c:[3],e:"Starting mid-tier and escalating based on evidence avoids both premature over-provisioning and under-provisioning."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"Claude Fable 5 and Claude Opus 5 are positioned differently in Anthropic's own guidance. Which best reflects that distinction?",o:["The two models are functionally identical, with no meaningful difference in recommended use case","Opus 5 for agentic coding and enterprise work; Fable 5 for the most demanding, longer-running workloads","Fable 5 is the cost-optimized option, while Opus 5 is reserved for latency-sensitive work","Opus 5 is positioned as strictly more capable than Fable 5 in every respect"],c:[1],e:"Anthropic's own guidance distinguishes Opus 5 as the agentic-coding/enterprise default from Fable 5 as the highest-capability option for the most demanding workloads."},
{d:"D5",t:"s",q:"When comparing two model tiers using an eval suite, why is a single run per tier usually insufficient to draw a confident conclusion?",o:["Because eval suites measure latency rather than output quality, so more runs are needed before comparing","Because eval suites are incapable of comparing more than one model at a time","Sampling-driven variability means a single run can look better or worse than typical, purely by chance","Because model comparisons are only valid when performed manually, without eval suites"],c:[2],e:"Given expected run-to-run variability, comparing tiers fairly usually calls for multiple runs rather than trusting a single sample from each."},
{d:"D5",t:"m",q:"Which two factors should primarily drive model tier selection for a given task? (Select 2)",o:["Which model was most recently released, independent of fit","Latency and cost requirements for that specific task","The developer's personal preference for one model's writing style","The task's actual complexity"],c:[1,3],e:"Complexity and latency/cost requirements are the substantive drivers; personal preference and recency aren't sound selection criteria on their own."},
{d:"D5",t:"m",q:"Which three statements about Claude Opus 5's effort levels are accurate? (Select 3)",o:["Opus 5 supports all five named effort levels, from low through max","The documented default effort level on Opus 5 is high","Opus 5 defaults to low effort unless explicitly overridden","Thinking cannot be disabled at xhigh or max effort on Opus 5"],c:[0,1,3],e:"Opus 5 supports the full low-to-max range, defaults to high, and blocks disabling thinking at xhigh/max — it does not default to low."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Over a very long agent session, instructions given at the very start gradually stop being followed even though nothing was explicitly changed. This is best described as:",o:["Context drift — a gradual loss of adherence to earlier instructions","Prompt injection, since some later input has overridden the original instructions","A vision-specific limitation unrelated to text instructions","A tool-use failure caused by a missing tool_result block"],c:[0],e:"Drift refers specifically to this gradual erosion of adherence to earlier instructions over a long session."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A long session accumulates large amounts of low-value exchange history that crowds out genuinely useful context. This is best described as:",o:["Prompt injection, which concerns untrusted content, not accumulated history","Context bloat — irrelevant accumulated history consuming context budget","Context drift, which specifically concerns fading adherence rather than crowding","Model hallucination, which concerns fabricated facts, not context volume"],c:[1],e:"Bloat is about volume and crowding, a distinct problem from drift even though both degrade a long session's quality."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A rule that should hold for an entire conversation — for example, 'always respond in formal English' — belongs in:",o:["The system prompt, since it's a stable, conversation-wide rule","A tool's description field, regardless of whether the rule involves tools","An MCP resource, which is meant for data access, not behavioral rules","The user turn, repeated fresh in every single message"],c:[0],e:"Stable, conversation-wide rules are exactly what the system prompt is meant to hold."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A one-off instruction relevant to only the current request — for example, 'summarize this in exactly three bullet points this time' — belongs in:",o:["An MCP prompt template, which is meant for reuse, not a one-off need","A project-level configuration file, unrelated to a single request","The system prompt, where it would persist across unrelated future turns","The current user turn, since it applies to this request specifically"],c:[3],e:"Task-specific, single-request instructions belong with that request, not in a persistent, conversation-wide location."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"What's the risk of putting a one-off, task-specific instruction into the system prompt instead of the current user turn?",o:["Little practical risk, since the model weights recent turns more heavily than older ones","It can bleed into unrelated later turns, since the system prompt persists across the session","It gets applied only to the very next turn and is then silently discarded by the model afterward","It will be silently ignored by the model regardless of where it's placed"],c:[1],e:"Because the system prompt persists, a one-off instruction placed there can wrongly keep applying to later, unrelated turns."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A subagent is dispatched to do broad, exploratory research with many dead ends, while a second subagent just needs to look up one deterministic fact. Which is the better candidate for context isolation?",o:["Both equally, since isolation provides the same benefit regardless of subtask shape","The deterministic lookup, since its single fixed answer benefits most from isolation","The exploratory research subtask, since isolation shields the main context from its noise","Whichever subtask runs longest, since elapsed time is what drives context growth"],c:[2],e:"Isolation pays off in proportion to how much intermediate noise a subtask generates, which a single fixed-answer lookup barely produces."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Requesting structured (for example, JSON) output from the model is valuable primarily because:",o:["It guarantees the response will always exactly match the requested schema","It removes the need for any validation of the response","It removes the need to handle variation, since the format constrains what can be produced","It supports more reliable downstream parsing, paired with actual validation"],c:[3],e:"Structure improves the odds of clean parsing, but validation is still necessary — it isn't a guarantee on its own."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"'Defensive parsing' of a model's output means:",o:["A technique that only applies when the response includes vision content","Handling malformed or unexpected output gracefully instead of assuming perfect compliance","Rejecting any response that fails validation and surfacing an error to the user immediately","Trusting that a structured-output request guarantees exact compliance every time"],c:[1],e:"Defensive parsing anticipates imperfect compliance rather than assuming a requested format is always honored exactly."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Including several worked examples in a prompt, rather than only describing the desired output format in words, is primarily useful because:",o:["Verbal descriptions of format are not supported by the API at all","Examples are cached separately from the rest of the prompt, which lowers their token cost","Examples consume fewer tokens than an equivalent verbal description of the same format","Concrete examples often communicate formatting and style more reliably than prose alone"],c:[3],e:"Few-shot examples tend to pin down format/style more concretely than prose description alone, which is their main value."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Iterative prompt refinement refers to:",o:["Settling the prompt during design and treating later changes as a sign of poor initial scoping","Adjusting a prompt over time based on real, observed failures and outputs","Refining only the model tier used, while leaving the prompt itself fixed","A process that only begins after a feature has publicly launched, with no earlier iteration"],c:[1],e:"Refinement is an ongoing, evidence-driven process grounded in what actually goes wrong in practice."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A prompt mostly performs well but consistently fails on one specific, identifiable edge case. What does this suggest?",o:["The prompt should be split into two, one for each class of input it receives","The edge case should be handled in application code rather than in the prompt","A few-shot example for that case should replace the prompt's written instructions","A targeted refinement addressing that specific case, rather than a full rewrite"],c:[3],e:"A narrow, reproducible failure calls for a narrow fix; the alternatives are heavier changes than the evidence justifies."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Guidance on exactly how a specific tool should behave when the model calls it is best placed:",o:["In the tool's input schema, as a description attached to each individual parameter","In the `tool_result` your application returns after the tool has already run","In the system prompt, so a single place governs how every tool behaves at once","In that tool's own description, where the model decides whether and how to call it"],c:[3],e:"The tool description is what the model reads when choosing a tool; parameter descriptions cover individual fields, and a tool_result arrives too late to guide the call."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Periodically summarizing older turns of a long session to free up context budget is a technique primarily aimed at managing:",o:["Prompt injection, which concerns untrusted content rather than volume","Model version pinning, which is unrelated to session history","Context bloat, by trimming the volume of accumulated low-value history","Context drift specifically, which concerns fading adherence rather than volume"],c:[2],e:"Summarization directly targets the volume problem that defines bloat."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Re-stating a critical constraint partway through a very long agent session is a practical mitigation for:",o:["Context drift, by re-anchoring an instruction that may be losing influence","Context bloat, which concerns accumulated volume rather than fading adherence","Output non-determinism, since repeating an instruction reduces run-to-run variation","Prompt cache invalidation, since restating changes the prefix that was cached"],c:[0],e:"Re-anchoring counters the gradual fading of an earlier instruction's influence, which is drift rather than volume or sampling variation."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A long agent session carries many old tool results that have since been superseded by newer ones. What technique specifically targets this?",o:["Prompt caching, which reuses a stable prefix rather than removing old content","Raising max_tokens, which affects the response rather than the accumulated input","Context editing, which removes stale tool results while leaving the rest intact","Switching model tiers, which changes capability rather than what context holds"],c:[2],e:"Context editing removes specific superseded content, a more targeted move than summarizing the conversation as a whole."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"On Claude Opus 5, a team lowers the effort level hoping to get shorter user-facing answers, but response length barely changes. What should they do instead?",o:["Enable prompt caching, which shortens output once the prefix is being reused","Lower the effort setting further, since length scales directly with that value","Switch to a smaller model, since lower-tier models naturally produce more concise answers","Ask for the desired length directly in the prompt, since effort governs thinking volume"],c:[3],e:"Effort controls how much thinking the model does rather than visible response length, so prompting for length is the documented approach."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A long Claude Opus 5 session needs a new standing instruction added partway through, without invalidating the prompt cache built up so far. What supports this?",o:["Restarting the session with a new system prompt that includes the instruction","Editing the top-level `system` field, which applies from the next turn onward","Appending a `{\"role\": \"system\"}` message to the end of the messages array","Adding the instruction to the tools array so it governs every subsequent call"],c:[2],e:"A mid-conversation system message carries the same authority as the top-level field but sits after the cached prefix, so it doesn't invalidate it; editing the top-level field would."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"Placing stable, reusable content — background docs, instructions, examples — at the very start of a prompt serves which two purposes together?",o:["It disables caching for that content, since only the final block can ever be cached","It gives the model a clear frame for the task and lets prompt caching reuse that content","It is required only when vision content blocks are present in the prompt","It has no effect on either clarity or caching, since ordering is purely cosmetic"],c:[1],e:"Prompt structure and caching mechanics reward the same habit: stable content up front, both for clarity and for cache reuse."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"'Compaction' of a long conversation's history refers to:",o:["Reducing the token cost of each request by switching to a more efficient tokenizer mid-session","A caching optimization that has no relationship to conversation history","Condensing older turns into a shorter representation that preserves essential information","Deleting the entire conversation history with no summary retained at all"],c:[2],e:"Compaction condenses rather than discards — the goal is to keep what matters while shrinking the footprint."},
{d:"D6",t:"s",q:"A tool's description reads 'use this tool for math,' but the tool actually only performs currency conversion. This is best characterized as a failure of:",o:["Prompt and tool-description clarity, likely to cause missed or incorrect tool use","Model tier selection, which is unrelated to how a tool is described","Model selection, since a more capable tier would infer the intended use from the name","Prompt caching, which is unrelated to what a description communicates"],c:[0],e:"A mismatched, vague description is a context/prompt-engineering clarity problem, not a tier or caching issue."},
{d:"D6",t:"m",q:"Which three are legitimate techniques for managing a long agent session's context health? (Select 3)",o:["Deliberately maximizing how much history accumulates in the main context","Periodically summarizing or pruning low-value older history","Isolating a noisy subtask's exploration into a separate subagent context","Re-anchoring a critical instruction partway through a long session"],c:[1,2,3],e:"Isolation, summarization/pruning, and re-anchoring are all genuine context-health techniques; deliberately maximizing accumulated history works against context health."},
{d:"D6",t:"m",q:"Which two subtasks are good candidates for context isolation via a subagent? (Select 2)",o:["Formatting an already-finalized answer for display","A noisy, multi-step tool-search process with a lot of trial and error","A single deterministic lookup with one known, fixed answer","A broad, open-ended research task with many dead ends"],c:[1,3],e:"Isolation earns its overhead on noisy, exploratory work; simple deterministic lookups and pure formatting have little noise worth isolating."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Three separate Claude-based internal assistants each need the same CRM access, and each is already an MCP-capable client. What is the most maintainable approach?",o:["A separate bespoke tool integration written and maintained inside each assistant","One MCP server exposing the CRM once, which all three assistants connect to","A nightly export of CRM records pasted into each assistant's system prompt","One shared assistant that the other two forward all of their CRM questions to"],c:[1],e:"When the consumers are already MCP clients, a single server standardizes the integration instead of duplicating it three times."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool's description field mainly serves to:",o:["Reduce token cost automatically, independent of the tool's actual use","Enable streaming for that specific tool's responses","Help the model decide when and how to correctly invoke that tool","Provide end-user-facing product documentation, unrelated to model behavior"],c:[2],e:"The description is read by the model itself when deciding whether and how to call the tool — it's not user-facing documentation."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"How does a built-in server tool such as web search differ from a tool an MCP server provides?",o:["Anthropic implements and runs the built-in tool; an MCP server is supplied and run by you","Built-in tools are limited to a fixed schema, while MCP tools can define arbitrary parameters","Built-in tools need an input schema, while MCP tools are described in prose alone","Built-in tools work only in testing, while MCP tools are the production equivalent"],c:[0],e:"The distinction is who implements and operates the tool, not capability, schema requirements, or environment."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Client-side tools execute:",o:["Within your own application's environment, not on Anthropic's infrastructure","Only when a request is submitted through the Message Batches API","Entirely on Anthropic's own servers, identical to server-side tools","Inside a sandboxed container that Anthropic provisions for each individual session"],c:[0],e:"The client-side/server-side distinction is defined by where execution actually happens — client-side runs in your own environment."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Server-side (or 'server') tools execute:",o:["On whichever machine the MCP server that defines them happens to be running at the time of the call","Within Anthropic's own infrastructure, rather than in the calling application's environment","Only when an MCP server is also connected to the same request","Only when a user explicitly triggers them, rather than when the model requests them"],c:[1],e:"Server tools are executed on Anthropic's side, which is the defining contrast with client-side tools."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An 'approval pattern' applied to a tool that sends payments means:",o:["The model declines payment-related requests outright rather than routing them for approval","A confirmation or human-in-the-loop step is required before the payment proceeds","The tool runs immediately, and a notification is sent to a reviewer afterwards for the record","The tool executes silently every time, with no additional review step"],c:[1],e:"An approval pattern gates the sensitive action behind a required confirmation, rather than removing the capability entirely."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP 'resource' primarily represents:",o:["Data that the server exposes for the model or client to read","A model tier setting applied to requests through that server","An executable action the model can trigger to change something","A billing configuration tied to the MCP server"],c:[0],e:"Resources are the data-access category in MCP, distinct from tools, which represent executable actions."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP 'prompt' primarily represents:",o:["An image content block passed through the MCP connection","A system prompt that the server enforces on every client which connects to it","A tool's error message returned after a failed call","A reusable prompt template that the server provides to connecting clients"],c:[3],e:"MCP prompts are reusable templates a server can hand to clients, a distinct category from both resources and tools."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP server intended to run as a local subprocess, communicating with the client over standard input and output, uses which transport?",o:["WebSocket","gRPC","stdio","Streamable HTTP"],c:[2],e:"MCP defines two standard transports, stdio and Streamable HTTP; stdio is the one used for a local subprocess. WebSocket and gRPC are not defined MCP transports."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Streamable HTTP, MCP's transport for servers that run as independent remote processes, is best understood as:",o:["A transport limited to local, same-machine communication only, like stdio","The transport that replaced the legacy SSE approach for remote MCP servers over HTTP","A vision-specific transport unrelated to how tools or resources are served","An older transport retained for local servers, since stdio superseded it for remote use"],c:[1],e:"Streamable HTTP is the current, defined transport for remote MCP servers, succeeding the earlier SSE-based approach."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An MCP server needs to be reachable by clients running on entirely different machines, not just a local process on the same machine as the client. Which transport fits that requirement?",o:["stdio, which is designed for a local subprocess sharing the client's machine","stdio, which can be pointed at a remote host by supplying that host's address in the configuration","Neither transport supports remote access under any circumstances","Streamable HTTP, since it supports remote server processes handling multiple client connections"],c:[3],e:"Remote reachability across machines is exactly the case Streamable HTTP is designed for, unlike the local-only stdio transport."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Skills differ from MCP servers primarily in that Skills:",o:["Are functionally identical to MCP servers, with no meaningful distinction between them","Package reusable instructions and procedural knowledge for the model itself","Only work when a remote server is available, unlike MCP","Cannot be used alongside MCP servers in the same session"],c:[1],e:"Skills are model-facing instruction packages; MCP servers are external-system connectors — different mechanisms serving different needs."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Agent Skills use a 'progressive disclosure' architecture. At the first level, what gets loaded into context for every available Skill, regardless of whether it's used?",o:["The Skill's entire SKILL.md file contents, in full, for every installed Skill","Nothing at all — no Skill content is loaded until it is explicitly invoked by name","Just its metadata — the name and description from the SKILL.md frontmatter","All of the Skill's bundled scripts and reference files, loaded upfront"],c:[2],e:"Only the lightweight metadata is always loaded; the fuller instructions and bundled resources load later, only when actually needed."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"Because only a Skill's metadata is loaded by default, what does this enable in practice?",o:["Only one Skill may ever be installed at a time, due to context limits","Many Skills can be installed simultaneously at minimal context cost","Skills must be manually loaded in full before every single request","Skills cannot bundle any scripts or reference files beyond SKILL.md itself"],c:[1],e:"Progressive disclosure is precisely what lets you install many Skills without paying their full context cost until one is actually relevant."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A capability that's common, already solved well, and needs zero custom build effort — such as general web search — is best sourced from:",o:["Extended thinking mode, which is unrelated to sourcing external capabilities","A brand-new MCP server, built from scratch for this specific application","A built-in tool, maintained and implemented by Anthropic","A brand-new custom tool, built from scratch for this specific application"],c:[2],e:"Built-in tools exist precisely to cover common, pre-solved needs without requiring custom build work."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool call fails because the external service it depends on times out. What should the integration do?",o:["Return a synthesized best-guess result so the agent loop continues uninterrupted","Retry the same call immediately, on the assumption the timeout was transient","Return a defined error result so the failure is surfaced and can be handled","Drop the tool from the request and let the model answer from its own knowledge"],c:[2],e:"An explicit, handled error keeps the model and the application honest about what happened; fabricating a result or silently dropping the tool hides the failure."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"A tool returns a large, loosely formatted blob of text that the model then has to interpret. What improves reliability?",o:["Returning a concise, structured result containing only the fields that matter","Returning the raw payload unchanged, so no potentially useful detail is lost","Returning nothing and letting the model infer the outcome from context","Returning the result as an image, which the model reads through vision"],c:[0],e:"Tool results occupy context and drive the next step, so a focused, structured result is more reliable than an unfiltered dump."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"An agent is given a single, extremely broad 'do anything' tool instead of several narrow, well-described tools. What's the main downside of that design?",o:["It automatically converts the tool into an MCP server","It improves flexibility, since one tool can cover cases you did not anticipate up front","It disables streaming for the rest of the session","Reduced predictability and harder-to-reason-about behavior"],c:[3],e:"An overly broad tool undermines both predictability and the ability to scope permissions tightly — a real, named downside."},
{d:"D8",t:"s",q:"The same MCP server needs to be used by both a command-line tool and a separate web application. What does MCP's standardization specifically enable here?",o:["Both clients can connect using the same protocol without needing fully custom, per-client integration work","Each client still needs to write its own adapter layer, though the server's underlying data model stays consistent","Only the command-line tool is actually able to connect to an MCP server","Nothing in particular — each client still needs a fully custom integration built from scratch"],c:[0],e:"Standardization is precisely what lets different kinds of clients reuse the same server without bespoke integration work each time."},
{d:"D8",t:"m",q:"Alongside tools, which two categories can an MCP server expose to connecting clients? (Select 2)",o:["Billing plans","UI theme settings","Resources","Prompts"],c:[2,3],e:"Resources and prompts are the two additional named categories MCP servers can expose beyond tools — billing and UI theming aren't part of the protocol."},
{d:"D8",t:"m",q:"Which three things should a well-written tool description clearly communicate? (Select 3)",o:["The internal org chart of the company that built it","What kind of result or output it returns","What inputs it expects","When the tool should be used"],c:[1,2,3],e:"Usage guidance, expected inputs, and expected output are the substantive content a tool description needs — an org chart is irrelevant to correct tool use."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Untrusted content retrieved from an external website is about to be fed into a prompt. What's the correct defensive approach?",o:["Add a system-prompt line politely asking the model to ignore any instructions found in that content","Trust the content fully, since Claude is assumed safe by default regardless of source","Disable tool use permanently for every future session as a precaution","Treat the content as untrusted, isolate it, and enforce guardrails against sensitive actions"],c:[3],e:"A polite request in the system prompt isn't an enforceable control; real defense requires isolation and guardrails around untrusted content."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"'Least privilege' applied to an agent's tool permissions means:",o:["Granting admin-level access by default, to be safe in case it's needed later","Granting a broad set of tools up front, then revoking any that go unused after review","Granting every available tool by default, for convenience","Granting only the specific tools and permissions strictly required for the task at hand"],c:[3],e:"Least privilege scopes an agent's permissions tightly to what the task actually requires — not more, and not zero."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"A Skill obtained from an unknown third party is about to be added to a production agent. What is the appropriate precaution?",o:["Review only its name and description, since those decide when it triggers","Audit its instructions and bundled scripts first, since a Skill can direct tool calls","Install it and watch production traffic, since problems will surface quickly","Rely on the model to disregard any instructions in it that look out of place"],c:[1],e:"A Skill can instruct the model to invoke tools and run code, so an untrusted one warrants the same scrutiny as installing third-party software."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Which practice reflects sound handling of API keys used to call Claude?",o:["Proxying calls through a backend, with the client supplying the key each time","Issuing one long-lived key per environment, rotated manually once a year","Committing the key encrypted, with the decryption key stored alongside it","Holding keys in server-side secret storage, out of prompts and client code"],c:[3],e:"Only the first keeps the credential off the client entirely; the others either extend exposure or still put the key in client hands."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"An attacker crafts a cleverly worded message attempting to get the model to bypass its safety behavior. What's the most robust way to defend a production agent against this?",o:["Writing a longer, more emphatic system prompt asking the model not to comply","Relying solely on the model's built-in safety training, with no additional application-level controls","Defense in depth: application-level guardrails layered alongside the model's own training","Disabling all logging, so any incidents are less visible to reviewers"],c:[2],e:"Multiple independent layers of defense are far more robust against jailbreak attempts than model training alone."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Where should content policy enforcement happen for a production application, beyond whatever the model itself does?",o:["Nowhere — content policy enforcement is unnecessary overhead for most applications","Only at the point where content is stored, since anything already generated has been checked","At the application layer as well, rather than relying solely on the model's own judgment","Only inside the model's training process, with no application-side checks at all"],c:[2],e:"Application-layer enforcement is an essential additional control point, not a redundant one."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"An agent has an unrestricted 'send email' tool, and a prompt-injected webpage instructs it to email an attacker-controlled address. What combination of failures does this illustrate?",o:["A prompt caching misconfiguration unrelated to tool permissions or content handling","A Message Batches API misconfiguration unrelated to real-time tool execution","A lack of least-privilege scoping combined with poor isolation of untrusted content","A vision-specific limitation that has nothing to do with email tools"],c:[2],e:"This scenario combines two separate, real failure modes: unscoped tool permissions and untrusted-content isolation."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Why are short-lived credentials generally preferred over long-lived static API keys for a production agent?",o:["They are cheaper to issue and rotate, which is the main reason teams tend to prefer them","They limit the window of exposure and potential damage if a credential is ever compromised","They are a strict requirement before streaming responses can be enabled","They directly improve the model's reasoning accuracy on the task"],c:[1],e:"The core benefit is a shorter exposure window, which limits how much damage a compromised credential can cause."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"An agent calls a third-party MCP server, and the text that server returns contains embedded instructions. How should that returned content be treated?",o:["As trusted content, because tool results are structurally exempt from injection","As irrelevant, because only content a user pastes can ever carry an injection","As trusted instruction, because it arrived through a configured, connected server","As untrusted input, since tool and server output can carry injected instructions too"],c:[3],e:"Indirect injection can arrive through any content the model reads, including tool and MCP server output, not just user-pasted text."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Why does least-privilege scoping matter specifically for an agent's tool permissions, beyond ordinary user-account permission hygiene?",o:["It matters less for agents, since a human reviews each tool call before it is executed","It doesn't matter any differently for agents than for any other kind of software account","An agent's tool choices can be influenced by injected content, so scoping limits the worst case","It fully replaces the need for any other security control on its own"],c:[2],e:"Because an agent's actions can be swayed by content it reads, tight scoping bounds the potential damage from that influence."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"After a security incident, a team needs to reconstruct exactly which tools an agent invoked and with what arguments. What makes this possible?",o:["Audit logging of tool calls and their arguments, captured as the agent runs","The model's own recollection of the session when asked about it afterward","Re-running the same prompt later and treating the new run as an accurate record","Prompt caching, which retains prior prompts in a reviewable stored form"],c:[0],e:"Only logging captured at execution time gives a dependable record; a re-run can diverge, and caching is not an audit trail."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"Storing an API key in a `.env` file that's excluded from version control and loaded only at runtime is an example of:",o:["A poor practice that should generally be avoided in favor of hardcoding","Reasonable, standard secret-management practice","A prompt-caching technique unrelated to how secrets are stored","An MCP resource definition, unrelated to secret handling"],c:[1],e:"Excluding secrets from version control and loading them at runtime is a widely accepted, sound baseline practice."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"A hook blocks a 'send email' tool call unless the recipient's domain matches an approved allowlist. What does this hook accomplish?",o:["A prompt-engineering pattern, since the allowlist is expressed within the tool's own description text rather than in code","A caching optimization applied specifically to outbound email requests","An enforceable guardrail that limits the potential blast radius if the agent is compromised or misled","An unnecessary restriction that should generally be removed to simplify the integration"],c:[2],e:"This is a concrete, enforceable guardrail — exactly the kind of control that bounds worst-case damage."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"An organization runs several teams under one Anthropic account and wants their prompt caches and API keys kept separate. What provides that separation?",o:["The effort parameter, set to a different named level for each individual team","Model version pinning, with each team assigned its own dated model snapshot","Workspaces, which scope keys and cache entries so they are not shared across teams","The Batch API, since batched requests are isolated from one another by design"],c:[2],e:"Workspace-level isolation is the mechanism that separates keys and cached content between teams within one organization."},
{d:"D7",t:"s",q:"A support assistant only needs a customer's order status, but the integration sends their full profile including payment details. What principle does this violate?",o:["Data minimization — sending only the data the task actually requires","Version pinning, which keeps model behavior stable across platform updates","Progressive disclosure, which defers loading content until it becomes relevant","Idempotency, which makes a repeated operation safe to execute more than once"],c:[0],e:"Sending more sensitive data than the task needs broadens exposure for no benefit, which is a data minimization failure."},
{d:"D7",t:"m",q:"Which two are enforceable technical controls against prompt injection risk? (Select 2)",o:["A politely worded system-prompt request asking the model to refuse injected instructions","Increasing max_tokens on the affected requests","Scoping tool permissions to least privilege","Isolating untrusted content from trusted instructions"],c:[2,3],e:"Content isolation and permission scoping are genuinely enforceable; a polite request and a token-limit change are not real technical controls against injection."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Claude Code's headless mode is best described as:",o:["A billing tier rather than a way of running the tool","A mode restricted to vision-based tasks exclusively","An interactive-only mode that requires constant human input at every step","A non-interactive, scriptable mode suited to automation and CI-style pipelines"],c:[3],e:"Headless mode is designed for non-interactive, scripted use rather than a live back-and-forth session."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"CLAUDE.md files can be nested within subdirectories of a project. What does that nesting primarily enable?",o:["Isolating that subdirectory completely, so root-level guidance no longer applies within it","Faster raw API response times for requests touching that subdirectory","More specific, subdirectory-level guidance layered on top of root-level context","Automatic pinning of the model version used within that subdirectory"],c:[2],e:"Nesting lets guidance get more specific closer to the code it applies to, cascading on top of root-level context."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"What does a Skill authored for Claude Code package together?",o:["A pinned model version that every session in the project is required to use","Billing configuration governing how the project's token spend is accounted for","The transport settings an MCP server needs in order to accept connections","Instructions and any supporting files for a recurring task, loaded when relevant"],c:[3],e:"A Skill bundles procedural instructions and supporting resources for a task, which is unrelated to billing, model pinning, or transports."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Where do custom Skills typically live when used in Claude Code?",o:["Stored only as environment variables, with no file-based representation","Embedded directly inside settings.json as inline JSON","Uploaded exclusively through a separate Skills API, with no filesystem-based option","As directories containing a SKILL.md file, in a personal or project-level folder"],c:[3],e:"Claude Code Skills are filesystem-based, living in personal or project-scoped folders that Claude reads directly."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Claude Code's 'Agent Memory' is best described as:",o:["A cache of raw API responses, unrelated to task context","A vision-processing feature unrelated to session context","Persisted context or notes carrying relevant information across sessions","An in-memory cache of recent API responses that is cleared when the session ends"],c:[2],e:"Agent Memory is about persisting useful context over time, not caching responses or tracking billing."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"A custom slash command in Claude Code is used to:",o:["Switch the session to a different model without changing anything else about it","Trigger a predefined, reusable action or prompt with a short command","Permanently delete the current project's files","Change the underlying model's training weights"],c:[1],e:"Slash commands are a shortcut for invoking a predefined, reusable action or prompt, not a destructive or model-altering operation."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"A developer enables Claude Code's `acceptEdits` permission mode for a session. What changes?",o:["Every action of any kind proceeds automatically with no restriction remaining","The session becomes read-only, since edits now require an external approval","All configured hooks are switched off for the remainder of that session","File edits proceed without a per-edit prompt, while other permissions still apply"],c:[3],e:"`acceptEdits` removes the per-edit confirmation step specifically; it doesn't lift other permission requirements or disable hooks."},
{d:"D3",t:"s",q:"Where should permission and tool-access configuration for a Claude Code project generally live?",o:["A separate MCP server, unrelated to local permission configuration","The system prompt exclusively, with no dedicated configuration file","settings.json","CLAUDE.md, which is meant for project guidance rather than permissions"],c:[2],e:"settings.json is the configuration home for permissions, hooks, and tool access, distinct from CLAUDE.md's guidance role."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A failure trace shows the model received malformed context because of a bug in how the application assembled the prompt. How should this be classified?",o:["A model regression, since the same prompt previously produced a usable answer","A prompt-engineering problem, to be addressed by rewording the instructions","A context-window overflow, since malformed context usually indicates truncation","An integration-layer bug, rather than a genuine model failure"],c:[3],e:"The defect is in the code assembling the prompt, so the fix belongs there; the alternatives are plausible misdiagnoses that would send the investigation elsewhere."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A team wants to know whether a prompt change improved or regressed quality before shipping it. What supports that judgment?",o:["A single side-by-side comparison run on one input the team found interesting","A production A/B test run on live traffic, with results reviewed after the change has shipped","A fixed set of representative cases scored the same way before and after the change","The subjective impression of whichever reviewer happens to read the output"],c:[2],e:"A stable, representative evaluation set scored consistently is what makes a before-and-after quality claim meaningful."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A regression appears in only 1 out of 20 runs of the exact same prompt. What should you consider before assuming it's a deterministic bug?",o:["Whether this is expected sampling-driven variability rather than a reproducible, deterministic defect","Whether the failing run happened to be routed to a different model snapshot than the others","That the API must be broken and should be reported immediately without further investigation","That the Message Batches API must be the cause, regardless of how the request was sent"],c:[0],e:"Low-frequency, intermittent variation is often expected non-determinism rather than a deterministic bug worth chasing as one."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A feature's costs are climbing and the team needs to find which requests are responsible. What should the integration record per request?",o:["Only the wall-clock duration, since latency and cost track one another closely","Only the final text of each response, which reflects what the spend produced","Token usage fields and a request identifier, logged alongside the calling context","Nothing extra, since per-request attribution is recoverable from the invoice"],c:[2],e:"Per-request usage figures plus an identifier are what let you attribute spend; latency and response text alone won't localize cost."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"A 'fallback strategy' for a failed model call might reasonably include:",o:["Silently ignoring the failure with no awareness of its impact on the user","Disabling logging so the issue becomes less visible going forward","Retrying with backoff, or falling back to a simpler deterministic path","Crashing immediately on any failure, so the problem surfaces rather than being masked"],c:[2],e:"A sound fallback strategy matches its response to the failure type, rather than defaulting to silence or a full crash."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"An eval suite built for an LLM feature should primarily measure:",o:["Only whether the surrounding code runs without throwing an exception","The total number of lines of code in the feature's implementation","Whether outputs meet defined quality and correctness criteria across representative cases","How quickly the feature returns a response when placed under representative production load"],c:[2],e:"Evals exist to check output quality against defined criteria, which goes well beyond just confirming the code executes."},
{d:"D4",t:"s",q:"Which is a good candidate for an automated check to run in CI for an LLM feature?",o:["A one-time check performed only before the feature's very first launch","A latency benchmark comparing the average response time against that of the previous release","A manual review of every single production request by a human reviewer","A structured-output schema-validation check run on sampled prompts on every change"],c:[3],e:"An automated, repeatable schema-validation check is exactly the kind of eval that fits naturally into a CI pipeline."},
];

if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) {
  module.exports = { DOMAINS, QUESTIONS };
}